      solution: `<p> (d)<br/> An air vessel in a reciprocating pump acts as a buffer or reservoir, which helps in smoothing out<br/> the flow rate and pressure variations caused by the piston 's reciprocating motion. During the<br/> pump 's stroke when flow is high, excess liquid enters the air vessel and compresses the air inside.<br/> When the flow is low, the compressed air expands and pushes liquid back into the pipe. This<br/> process maintains a nearly uniform flow rate and provides a continuous supply of the fluid. This<br/> also leads to other benefits such as reduced acceleration head, lower frictional losses, and the<br/>ability to run the pump at higher speeds. </p>`,
