      answer: "D",
      solution: `<p> (d)<br/> In secondary wastewater treatment, the biomass growth follows a typical bacterial growth curve<br/> with distinct phases:<br/> •<strong>Lag phase:</strong> Initially, the bacteria adapt to the new environment and prepare for rapid growth,<br/> with minimal cell division.<br/> •<strong>Log phase (exponential phase):</strong> The bacteria actively divide and rapidly increase in number<br/> due to optimal conditions.<br/> •<strong>Stationary phase:</strong> The population growth slows down as the nutrient availability becomes<br/> limited, and the rate of cell birth equals the rate of cell death.<br/> •<strong>Endogenous phase:</strong> In some cases, after the stationary phase, there might be a decline in the<br/>population due to nutrient depletion or accumulation of waste products. </p>`,
