      answer: "C",
      solution: `<p> (c)<br/> The shape of isochrones is determined by the conditions that govern the dissipation of excess<br/> pore water pressure during consolidation<br/> <strong>•Initial distribution of excess pore pressure:</strong> The shape of the initial isochrone (at time t=0)<br/> reflects the initial excess pore pressure distribution caused by the applied load. For a uniformly<br/> distributed load, this is often a parabola, which then changes over time.<br/> <strong>•Drainage conditions (one way or two way):</strong> The drainage conditions dictate where water<br/> can escape from the soil sample. This profoundly affects the isochrone 's shape, as excess pore<br/>pressure is zero at the drainage boundaries thereby causing the curve to flatten from those points over time. </p>`,
