      answer: "A",
      solution: `<p> (a)<br/> •The Streeter-Phelps equation is a classic water quality model used to predict the concentration of dissolved oxygen downstream from a source of pollution. It is derived from a mass balance approach, considering two primary competing processes:<br/> 1. Deoxygenation 2. Reaeration<br/> •The relationship between water temperature and dissolved oxygen saturation is inverse.<br/> Cold water has a higher capacity to hold dissolved gases, including oxygen, than warm<br/> water. For example, the saturation concentration of DO in fresh water at 0 °C is about 14.6 mg/<em>l</em> ,<br/> while at 30 °C it is about 7.6 mg/<em>l</em> . This is why thermal pollution (discharging hot water into<br/>a river) can be harmful to aquatic life, as it reduces the available dissolved oxygen. </p>`,
